{
  "thinking": "I’ll test five explanations. An honest mix of separate customer runs calls for checking customer records and whether the interconnect shows distinct workloads. A large batch inference job calls for comparing outbound traffic, interconnect traffic, and live chip probes where available. One training run split across jobs or billing accounts calls for checking cross-cluster traffic, sealed job records, and customer independence. Training on unlisted hardware calls for comparing manufacturer inventory and grid energy with the operator’s system list. Missing logs or late payments call for checking escrow, bank records, and customer replies. I’ll start with the independent hardware inventory.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "I’ll start with the manufacturer’s inventory and compare it with the operator’s system list. I’ll test four explanations: (1) one oversized training run was split across job labels or clusters, so I’ll look for sustained peer traffic across those boundaries and estimate GPU-hours from corroborated counts and duration. (2) Separate customers ran independent training jobs, so I’ll check customer identities and look for isolated traffic. (3) Batch inference or cooling made power look like training, so I’ll compare the power signal with peer traffic and probe live GPUs if the workload is still active. (4) Missing logs or late payments created discrepancies in an otherwise compliant month, so I’ll compare escrowed logs, utility energy and bank records.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Competing explanations: (1) separate customers ran independent jobs below the cap. Compare the sealed logs, customer records, and cross-cluster traffic. (2) A large batch-inference job ran at steady power. Use the interconnect tap to distinguish inference from training and a live chip probe if the workload is still running. (3) Missing logs or late payments made legitimate jobs look suspicious. Compare the escrow and bank records with independent usage evidence. (4) One training run exceeded the cap under several job labels. Check whether the tap shows sustained GPU traffic across the claimed job boundaries, then use balance checks to assess GPU-hours. (5) Training used GPUs omitted from the site list. Compare manufacturer inventory with the operator's list and check for unexplained grid usage. Start with the independent inventory.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Competing explanations and checks: (1) Independent customers ran legitimate jobs below the cap. Check customer registrations and replies, and sample cross-cluster traffic for coordination. (2) A long batch inference job resembled training. Compare outbound traffic and peer traffic, and probe representative GPUs if the job is still active. (3) One training run was split across job labels or customers. Check for sustained cross-cluster traffic and use the balance checks to assess its GPU-hours. (4) Training ran on unlisted hardware or links. Compare manufacturer inventory with the system list and reconcile grid energy with recorded workloads. (5) Missing logs or late payments created innocent discrepancies. Compare scheduler records with escrow and check the timing against bank records and customer replies. Start with manufacturer inventory to establish the independent hardware baseline.",
  "action": "inventory",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "Compare the operator’s cluster counts and serial ranges with the 2,000 GPUs shipped to Eemshaven. The system list will also identify which feeds and links can be checked independently.",
  "action": "system_list",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The manufacturer confirms delivery of 2,000 GPUs. Compare that baseline with the operator’s list to map clusters, feeds, and links for independent checks. If the counts differ, check whether the gap reflects hardware that was out of service or unlisted capacity.",
  "action": "system_list",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The manufacturer confirms 2,000 GPUs were delivered to Eemshaven. Compare the operator’s system list with those shipments to identify where the GPUs are accounted for and whether any listed systems or links need independent verification.",
  "action": "system_list",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The listed clusters account for all 2,000 GPUs in the manufacturer’s shipment records. I’ll check the grid operator’s energy records for sustained load or discrepancies, then compare the timing with the escrowed scheduler logs.",
  "action": "utility_energy",
  "args": {
    "first_day": 1,
    "last_day": 30
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The two listed clusters account for all 2,000 GPUs in the shipment records. Request the independent grid energy record for all 30 days so the balance checks can compare site consumption with recorded workloads and flag periods for closer inspection.",
  "action": "utility_energy",
  "args": {
    "first_day": 1,
    "last_day": 30
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "F1 has higher load on days 3–5 and 14–15, while F2 has higher load on days 8–9. I’ll compare those windows with the sealed scheduler records and look for jobs that span dates or clusters. The escrow can establish what was recorded at the time, and independent measurements can test those entries.",
  "action": "log_escrow",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The escrow rules out later edits to the daily logs. Read the matching scheduler records to identify which jobs account for the three grid energy increases and which cluster links to sample for coordination.",
  "action": "scheduler_log",
  "args": {
    "first_day": 1,
    "last_day": 30
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The first two jobs total 50,400 recorded GPU-hours if they were one coordinated run. Escrow confirms the entries were filed contemporaneously, but it cannot establish who commissioned the work or whether the second job resumed the first. I’ll check the first customer’s registration and ask what workload it commissioned.",
  "action": "customer_check",
  "args": {
    "customer": "Tidewater Robotics"
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Utility records show three higher-load windows. Outbound traffic rose only on days 14–15, which supports an inference explanation for part of that load. I’ll compare the scheduler’s job labels, GPU counts, and timing across both clusters to identify where independent measurements can test whether jobs were separate or coordinated.",
  "action": "scheduler_log",
  "args": {
    "first_day": 1,
    "last_day": 30
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The balance checks support the recorded workload but leave the day 14 batch label unverified. Sample cluster A during that job. Inference-like peer traffic would support the label, while substantially higher traffic would warrant investigating training and its duration.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 14
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "A’s day 15 traffic strongly favors batch inference over either training profile described. The tap samples listed links on one day. Check F1 on the same day for a periodic power signal that could warrant further investigation. Batch inference and cooling can also produce a line.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {"feed": "F1", "day": 15}
}

{
  "thinking": "The logged jobs line up with the three higher-load windows, and outbound traffic rose during the reported batch job. I’ll run the fixed balance checks to see whether the recorded workloads account for the independently metered energy, including on days 11–13, and use any discrepancy to target an independent measurement.",
  "action": "balance_checks",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The bank confirms that Tidewater and Ostara paid matching invoices from their own accounts. That supports separate commissions and leaves open whether the two jobs shared training state. I’ll run the fixed balance checks to see whether the grid, inventory, scheduler, and payment records reveal discrepancies that need independent follow-up.",
  "action": "balance_checks",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The sealed records say no jobs were scheduled on days 11–13, and both GPU feeds stayed near their usual load. I’ll sample Cluster A’s GPU-to-GPU traffic on day 12 to test whether the missing logs reflect a storage failure during routine inference or conceal training within the usual power load. I’ll check the unpaid Lumen Health invoice against the bank records separately.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 12
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The bank records support separate commissions, but the two F1 jobs would exceed the threshold by 400 GPU-hours if they shared training state. I’ll measure the first job’s power signature on a full day so I can compare it with the second job’s signature. Similar frequencies alone would carry little weight.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F1",
    "day": 6
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The tap readings on days 15 and 17 favor batch inference. A training phase on days 12–14 at the logged allocation of 700 GPUs would reach 50,400 GPU-hours. Sample day 13 to test that possibility independently of the scheduler label.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 13
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "A’s traffic on days 13, 15, and 17 favors batch inference. A training phase on unsampled days remains possible. The recorded B fine-tune is below the cap, all daily logs reached escrow, and the listed GPU count matches manufacturer shipments. Run the fixed balance checks for discrepancies in energy, allocation, and billing. Payment gaps can reflect late billing, while unexplained load can guide independent checks for concealed training.",
  "action": "balance_checks",
  "args": {}
}

{
  "thinking": "The outbound shortfall on days 11–15 and equal surplus on days 16–20 could reflect delayed batch delivery, changes in interactive demand, or training concealed within the batch allocation. The tap favors inference on the three sampled days. Use the provider’s daily records to see whether the traffic shifts align with unsampled days or the end of the batch job.",
  "action": "outbound_traffic",
  "args": {
    "first_day": 1,
    "last_day": 30
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The bank confirms that Lumen Health has paid nothing, and the scheduler lists no job under its name. I’ll check the company register and ask Lumen about the service and payment timing to distinguish an ordinary unpaid invoice from a possible unrecorded workload.",
  "action": "customer_check",
  "args": {
    "customer": "Lumen Health"
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Both within-cluster taps showed inference-level traffic on day 22. Shift to an independent power measurement on F1 during day 28, a live audit day with no scheduled job and ordinary outbound traffic. A periodic line could come from training, batch inference, or cooling. If one appears, compare it across days and use a live chip probe to help distinguish those explanations.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {"feed": "F1", "day": 28}
}

{
  "thinking": "Outbound traffic stayed near baseline through day 18, then jumped to 45.4 TB on the last day of the batch allocation. That fits a single batch delivery, but the provider's totals cannot identify the recipient or establish what produced the data. Check Meridian Analytics' registration and ask whether it confirms the workload and delivery timing.",
  "action": "customer_check",
  "args": {
    "customer": "Meridian Analytics"
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Separate payments, customer replies, and different F1 power signatures favor three separate fine-tunes. The daily energy totals still leave room for training within the load attributed to inference. I’ll sample Cluster A on a day with no declared fine-tune to check whether GPU-to-GPU traffic fits the reported workload.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 27
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Days 16–21 have no declared scheduled jobs and no independent spectrum or tap samples. Check F1 on day 18. A periodic line would give us a day to test for elevated peer traffic, which would help distinguish undeclared training from periodic inference or cooling.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F1",
    "day": 18
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Meridian's account of a single end-of-job download matches the provider's day 19 traffic spike. Independent tap readings on three days strongly favor inference, and the site-wide records show no unexplained load. Taken together, the evidence favors no violation by more than four to one.",
  "action": "report",
  "args": {
    "verdict": "NO_VIOLATION",
    "confidence": 95,
    "integrity": "CLEAN",
    "finding": "The evidence supports batch inference for the 134,400 GPU-hour allocation. The recorded fine-tune used 7,200 GPU-hours.",
    "key_evidence": [
      {
        "what": "Cluster A's GPU-to-GPU traffic was consistent with inference on days 13, 15, and 17, including when compared with low-communication training.",
        "source": "interconnect_tap",
        "vouched_by": "Verifier's optical tap"
      },
      {
        "what": "Outbound traffic stayed near baseline through day 18 and rose to 45.4 TB on day 19, the final active day of the batch allocation.",
        "source": "outbound_traffic",
        "vouched_by": "Internet provider"
      },
      {
        "what": "Meridian Analytics confirmed ordering batch inference on 700 GPUs and collecting the results in one download at the end. Its registration showed an established company at its own address.",
        "source": "customer_check",
        "vouched_by": "Company register and Meridian Analytics"
      },
      {
        "what": "Metered energy matched declared workloads within 10% on all checked feed-days, and the 2,000 listed GPUs matched manufacturer shipments.",
