  "args": {
    "link": "A",
    "day": 4
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Day 8 traffic supports a pause between the two F1 jobs and leaves a checkpoint restart possible. Separate payments and different power signatures favor separate runs. I’ll check F1 on day 17, when grid load was typical and no fine-tune was declared, for a periodic signal from possible training on links outside the tap’s coverage.",
  "action": "power_spectrum",
  "args": {
    "feed": "F1",
    "day": 17
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The energy balance fits both batch inference and training. The day 26 tap strongly favors training, but its aggregate cannot assign the traffic to a job. Probe GPUs spread across the logged batch allocation during the surge, with two GPUs from the interactive pool for comparison. The probe can test the workload label. Whether the GPUs belong to one coordinated run remains open.",
  "action": "chip_probe",
  "args": {
    "nodes": ["A-0147", "A-0392", "A-0527", "A-0586", "A-0643", "A-0709", "A-0774", "A-0831", "A-0898", "A-0956", "A-1019", "A-1087", "A-1142", "A-1191"],
    "hour": 610
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "Cluster A’s day 4 traffic of about 106 GB/s fits the declared 100-GPU fine-tune. On day 9, Cluster B’s higher power could reflect the declared 150-GPU fine-tune or a larger unrecorded workload. I’ll use the independent tap to check whether peer traffic fits the roughly 150 GB/s expected for the declared job.",
  "action": "interconnect_tap",
  "args": {
    "link": "B",
    "day": 9
  }
}

{
  "thinking": "The balance of evidence favors separate fine-tunes. The two F1 jobs would total 50,400 GPU-hours if coordinated, but separate payer accounts, distinct power signatures, and inference-level traffic during the gap support treating them as separate runs. Independent energy and traffic records provide no affirmative evidence of an undeclared run above the threshold.",
  "action": "report",
